WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 291: Cnut the Great: The North Sea Empire, the Danegeld, and the Waves of the Sea

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:14.880
[Rebecca] Today, our Kings and Queens of England series explores the remarkable Danish conqueror, who became one of England's wisest and most devout monarchs.

4
00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:26.720
[Rebecca] As we examine his rule, we will uncover the true historical meaning behind the famous legend of the king commanding the incoming tide, showing it not as vanity, but as an act of profound humility.

5
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:33.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the extraordinary life of King Seanut the Great.

6
00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:43.280
[George] Born around 995, C Nut was the second son of King Swain Forkbeard of Denmark and a Slavic princess named Gunhild of Poland.

7
00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:43.520
[George] C.

8
00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:56.480
[George] Nut's youth was spent in an era of apocalyptic Viking resurgence against Anglo-Saxon England, ruled by the ineffective and indecisive King Ethelred I, notoriously remembered as Ethelred the Unready.

9
00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:10.720
[George] Incapable of fielding disciplined armies, Ethelred repeatedly levied crushing taxes known as Danegeld upon his people, paying hundreds of thousands of silver pounds to buy off Danish invaders, which merely whetted their appetite for more.

10
00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:18.320
[George] In 1013, Swain Forkbeard launched a full-scale invasion of conquest, bringing teenage Seanut to England.

11
00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:26.880
[George] Swain drove Ethelred into exile in Normandy and was proclaimed King of England, only to drop dead suddenly in February 1014.

12
00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:34.800
[George] The Danish fleet proclaimed 19-year-old Seanut their king, initiating a titanic three-year war for the mastery of England.

13
00:01:34.800 --> 00:01:40.480
[George] The devastating Danegeld extortions of the late 10th century had bled the English realm white.

14
00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:57.120
[George] King Ethelred the Unready paid £24,000 of silver in 1002, £36,000 in 1007, and a staggering 48,000 pounds in 1012, yet each payment only financed larger and more audacious Viking fleets.

15
00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:11.640
[George] Growing up in the warrior society of gelling in Denmark, young Seanut was schooled in the brutal martial arts of the Danish Scalds, learning to wield the heavy two-handed Dane axe and navigate the stormy waters of the Baltic and North Seas.

16
00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:29.560
[George] Swain Forkbeard's conquest of England in 1013 was a ruthless military campaign of terror, burning villages, looting abbeys, and marching through Mercia, Swain taught young Seanut that power was fragile unless anchored by unquestioned military dominance and institutional legitimacy.

17
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:46.280
[George] Returning in 1015 with an armada of 200 dragonships whose gilded dragon prows gleamed in the sun, Seanut was confronted by a fierce new adversary, Ethelred's eldest son, Edmund Ironside, a warrior prince of ferocious courage and physical strength.

18
00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:52.840
[George] Throughout 1016, Seanut and Edmund fought five Titanic pitched battles across southern England.

19
00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:59.560
[George] On October 18, 1016, the decisive clash took place at the Battle of Asandon in Essex.

20
00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:09.960
[George] Just as the Saxon lines were holding, the treacherous Saxon Eeldorman Edric Striona deserted the field with his men, handing Seanut a total, crushing victory.

21
00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:16.040
[George] Meeting on the Isle of Alny in the River Severn, the two exhausted warriors agreed to divide England.

22
00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:20.280
[George] Edmund kept Wessex, while Seanut took Mercia and the North.

23
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:29.560
[George] When Edmund died mysteriously six weeks later in November 1016, the English Whiton unanimously elected Seanut as king of all England.

24
00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:39.080
[George] The epic duel between 19-year old Seanut and Edmund Ironside was an extraordinary clash of titans that captivated both Saxon and Norse chroniclers.

25
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.920
[George] Five major battles were fought across England in a single summer.

26
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:48.640
[George] With Edmund repeatedly breaking Danish sieges of London with daring cavalry relief columns.

27
00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.000
[George] At a Sandon, fought on a chalk ridge in Essex, the slaughter was catastrophic.

28
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:07.600
[George] The Saxon shield wall held for hours until the treacherous Saxon Eeldorman Edric Striona turned his troops and fled, allowing Seanut's flanking cavalry to envelop the Saxon army and cut down the cream of the English nobility.

29
00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:07.920
[George] C.

30
00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:13.920
[George] Nut's subsequent peace treaty with Edmund on the Isle of Alny demonstrated his pragmatic statesmanship.

31
00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:21.840
[George] Recognizing Edmund's formidable military skill, Cnut agreed to divide the kingdom rather than risk a protracted war of attrition.

32
00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:22.160
[George] C.

33
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.240
[George] Nut proved to be a political realist of extraordinary shrewdness.

34
00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:31.360
[George] To legitimate his rule and neutralize the threat of a Norman-backed Saxon rebellion, C.

35
00:04:31.360 --> 00:04:37.680
[George] Nut sent for Ethelred's widow, Queen Emma of Normandy, daughter of Duke Richard I of Normandy.

36
00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:49.120
[George] Emma, an astute and formidable political operator in her own right, agreed to marry the Danish conqueror on the condition that any sons born of their union would take precedence in the English succession over C.

37
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:54.000
[George] Nut's children by his English handfast wife, Aylfjifu of Northampton.

38
00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:06.400
[George] To purge potential traitors, C Nutt famously summoned the Turncoat Eildorman Edric Striona to his palace, asking him how he could remain faithful to a Danish master when he had betrayed his own Saxon kin.

39
00:05:06.400 --> 00:05:08.080
[George] When Edric stammered, C.

40
00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:10.560
[George] Nut ordered his execution on the spot.

41
00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:13.200
[George] Proving that treachery earned only death.

42
00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:13.520
[George] C.

43
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.400
[George] Nutt's marriage to Queen Emma of Normandy was a masterclass in political alliance.

44
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:21.680
[George] By marrying the widow of his predecessor Ethelred, C.

45
00:05:21.680 --> 00:05:32.200
[George] Nut immediately legitimized his claim to the Saxon throne, won the allegiance of the English church, and neutralized the threat of an invasion from Emma's brother, Duke Richard I.

46
00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.480
[George] of Normandy.

47
00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:34.120
[George] While C.

48
00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:45.480
[George] Nut kept his first English consort, Aylfjifu of Northampton, as his northern regent, Emma reigned as his official Christian queen in Winchester, bearing him a son named Harthicnut.

49
00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:46.040
[George] When C.

50
00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:54.840
[George] Nut executed the treacherous turncoat Edric Striona during Christmas festivities at Westminster Palace, ordering his body cast into the Thames.

51
00:05:54.840 --> 00:06:02.440
[George] English chroniclers praised the Danish king for showing that those who betray their lawful sovereigns would find no honor under his rule.

52
00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:13.720
[George] It was during Seanut's peaceful reign that English folklore recorded the immortal story of Seanut and the waves of the sea, first documented in the 12th century by Henry of Huntingdon.

53
00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:32.840
[George] Popular memory often mistakenly portrays Seanut as a foolish, megalomoniacal tyrant who arrogantly believed he could stop the tides, but historical analysis reveals that the episode was a deliberately staged, brilliant piece of royal theatre designed to teach his obsequious courtiers a lesson in Christian humility.

54
00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:47.560
[George] Disgusted by sycophantic courtiers who constantly flattered him, claiming that his royal power was absolute, and that even the elements obeyed his command, C Nut ordered his royal throne placed upon the seashore as the ocean tide was rushing in.

55
00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:51.800
[George] Sitting in his royal robes, C Nut commanded the surging water.

56
00:06:51.800 --> 00:06:57.000
[George] Thou, too, art subject to my dominion, and the land on which I sit is mine.

57
00:06:57.000 --> 00:06:59.880
[George] I command thee not to flow over my land.

58
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:02.680
[George] Nor presume to whet the feet of thy sovereign.

59
00:07:02.680 --> 00:07:15.200
[George] When the relentless waves broke over his chair and soaked his legs, C Nut turned to his silent, trembling courtiers, proclaiming, Let all the world know that the power of earthly kings is vain and empty.

60
00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:20.400
[George] For none is worthy of the name of king save he whose gnawed heaven, earth, and sea obey.

61
00:07:20.880 --> 00:07:28.240
[George] Taking off his gold crown, Seanut hung it upon a crucifix in Winchester Cathedral, never wearing it on his head again.

62
00:07:28.240 --> 00:07:44.800
[George] The 12th-century chronicler Henry of Huntingdon, who first recorded the episode of Seanut and the Waves, explicitly framed the story not as an act of regal folly, but as an intentional, public demonstration of profound Christian piety and theological orthodoxy.

63
00:07:44.800 --> 00:08:00.720
[George] In an era when court poets and royal counsellors showered monarchs with blasphemous flattery, comparing their earthly authority to the divine majesty of God, Seanut used the relentless power of the ocean tide to dramatically expose the pathetic limits of mortal kingship.

64
00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:16.320
[George] By hanging his golden crown upon the great crucifix above the high altar of Winchester Cathedral, Seanut made a public confession of faith that resonated across Christian Europe, that all earthly crowns are brittle, tranchant dust before the sovereign king of kings.

65
00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:22.960
[George] C-nut was now the undisputed architect of a maritime superpower known to history as the North Sea Empire.

66
00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:30.960
[George] When his brother King Harald I I of Denmark died in 1018, C-nut sailed to Scandinavia to claim the Danish crown.

67
00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:44.720
[George] In 1028, with an armada of fifty ships and massive gold bribes to Norwegian chieftains, Seanut drove King Olaf Haraldsson out of Norway, claiming the Norwegian throne and adding parts of Sweden to his dominion.

68
00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.080
[George] Ruling from London and Winchester.

69
00:08:46.800 --> 00:08:52.120
[George] Seanut governed his vast trilateral realm with astonishing administrative harmony.

70
00:08:52.400 --> 00:09:09.800
[George] He did not impose Danish law on England, but codified Anglo-Saxon legal customs, restored monastic lands, and appointed both Saxon and Danish earls, most notably elevating Godwin to the Earldom of Wessex, founding the dynasty that would define 1066.

71
00:09:09.800 --> 00:09:17.480
[George] C Nut's North Sea Empire was an astonishing geopolitical achievement, ruling over England, Denmark, and Norway.

72
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:30.200
[George] Seanut effectively controlled the lucrative commercial trade routes of the Baltic, North Sea and English Channel, creating a peaceful maritime commonwealth where merchants could travel unmolested from Bergen to Bristol.

73
00:09:30.200 --> 00:09:41.320
[George] He governed England through four great earldoms, Wessex, Mercia, East Anglia and Northumbria, relying on loyal Saxon statesmen like Earl Leofric and Earl Godwin.

74
00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:47.960
[George] In his monumental law codes issued at Winchester, drafted with the assistance of Archbishop Wolfston of York.

75
00:09:47.960 --> 00:10:00.920
[George] Seanut reaffirmed the traditional laws of King Edgar the Peaceful, strictly protecting English property rights, forbidding the sale of Christian slaves overseas, and punishing corrupt judges with ruthless severity.

76
00:10:00.920 --> 00:10:08.920
[George] In the spring of 1027, at the zenith of his imperial power, C-nut undertook a solemn personal pilgrimage to Rome.

77
00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:12.120
[George] Attending the coronation of Holy Roman Emperor Conrad I.

78
00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:12.680
[George] at St.

79
00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:19.880
[George] Peter's Basilica alongside Pope John Xiax, Seanut was treated with the highest honour by European royalty.

80
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:25.720
[George] Displaying his diplomatic skill, Seanut negotiated valuable commercial concessions.

81
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:38.680
[George] He secured reductions in the onerous transit tolls imposed on English and Danish merchants and pilgrims crossing the Alps, and agreed to a marriage alliance between his daughter Gunhilda and Emperor Conrad's son Henry.

82
00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:44.840
[George] In an open letter dispatched to the people of England from Rome, Seanut wrote with touching sincerity.

83
00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:51.600
[George] I have dedicated my life to God, to rule my kingdoms with justice and to atone for the errors of my youth.

84
00:10:51.600 --> 00:10:55.760
[George] I have never shrunk from labor to promote the welfare of my subjects.

85
00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:56.080
[George] C.

86
00:10:56.160 --> 00:11:05.360
[George] Nut's pilgrimage to Rome in 1027 was the crowning diplomatic triumph of his reign, walking through the gates of Rome in the robes of a humble penitent.

87
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:05.680
[George] C.

88
00:11:05.680 --> 00:11:11.600
[George] Nut was received with imperial honors by Pope John Xiax and Holy Roman Emperor Conrad I.

89
00:11:11.600 --> 00:11:17.200
[George] I, standing as an equal brother sovereign at Conrad's magnificent imperial coronation.

90
00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:17.520
[George] C.

91
00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:32.720
[George] Nut leveraged his prestige to secure sweeping commercial treaties that abolished arbitrary tolls on English and Danish merchants crossing the Alpine passes, and secured direct reductions in the heavy pallium fees demanded of English archbishops visiting the Holy See.

92
00:11:32.720 --> 00:11:45.200
[George] In his open letter sent to England, C-nut declared his solemn vow to govern with justice and mercy, demonstrating that the Viking conqueror had evolved into one of the most civilized Christian statesmen of Europe.

93
00:11:45.200 --> 00:12:02.400
[George] Passing away peacefully at Shaftesbury on November 12, 1035, around the age of forty, C Nut was buried in the Old Minster at Winchester alongside ancient Saxon kings, his bones resting in painted mortuary chests that remain in Winchester Cathedral to this day.

94
00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:15.200
[George] Without Seanut's magnetic personal genius and diplomatic authority, his vast North Sea Empire dissolved almost immediately amid bitter succession squabbles between his sons, Harold Harefoot and Harthicnut.

95
00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.320
[George] Yet Seanut's legacy in England was monumental.

96
00:12:18.320 --> 00:12:33.160
[George] He proved that an alien conqueror could rule with profound wisdom, justice, and piety, showing that the true greatness of a Christian sovereign lies not in commanding the waves, but in humbly bowing before the eternal laws of God and the welfare of his people.

97
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:39.400
[George] King Seanut's death at Shaftesbury in November 1035 brought mourning across England.

98
00:12:39.400 --> 00:12:50.200
[George] The Danish conqueror had given the English realm nearly two decades of uninterrupted peace, prosperity, and internal security after a half century of devastating Viking warfare.

99
00:12:50.200 --> 00:13:01.800
[George] His body was laid to rest in the old Minster at Winchester, where his bones were later placed in the painted mortuary chests that still rest atop the stone presbytery screens of Winchester Cathedral.

100
00:13:01.800 --> 00:13:17.960
[George] King Senut the Great proved that true sovereign greatness is defined not by ethnicity or origin, but by the wisdom, humility, and justice with which a monarch governs, leaving an immortal legacy as the Viking who became England's wise and devout protector.

101
00:13:18.520 --> 00:13:21.480
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

102
00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:29.800
[Rebecca] King Sean the Great's wise governance, maritime empire, and lesson in royal humility mark him as one of England's most capable monarchs.

103
00:13:29.800 --> 00:13:41.720
[Rebecca] Join us next week as Rebecca returns with the pious, fateful reign of Edward the Confessor, the building of Westminster Abbey, and the disputed succession that sparked the Norman conquest of 1066.

104
00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:44.200
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

